Use a gas model with defined pressure, temperature, volume, and composition (@gc-gases: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F", #general-chemistry "Molecular-motion-and-distributions.lingua#r_44XEP1GDGE3QKRCSEXBZM135TY", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/properties/Equations-of-state-and-caloric-information.lingua#r_3YYAN5FQJXPX2441B2ZK5M4VPN") { r_6SXC8H8YSR9B739R9H8K2KF863
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 9 in full with exercises; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the complete six-part gas unit; [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), pressure, temperature, gas, and composition conventions.

The ideal-gas equation pV = nRT connects a state under a model whose validity needs assessment. Use absolute pressure and thermodynamic temperature, consistent units for R, and the amount of the specified gas entities. A gauge reading and an absolute pressure are different quantities; a Celsius temperature must be converted before substituting into this equation.

For an ideal mixture, component partial pressure is p_i = y_i p and amounts add. Specify whether a collected gas contains water vapor or another component. The pressure measured for the mixture is not automatically the partial pressure of the species whose amount is sought. A chemical reaction can change total gas amount while conserving elements.

Under hypothetical p = 100.0 kPa, V = 24.78957 L, and T = 298.15 K, the ideal model gives approximately one mole using compatible SI R. These are teaching inputs, not a universal molar volume. Changing temperature, pressure, or gas nonideality changes the volume/amount relation.

**Work:** derive density and molar-mass relations from the ideal equation, checking units. Account for a stated binary mixture and a wet-gas collection problem. Compare a constant-temperature compression with a process in which temperature changes, identifying what additional information is needed.

Read the entire gas chapter and Chem1 gas unit. An engineering vessel also needs material, pressure, thermal, flow, and safety design beyond this state equation. Here establish the chemical-state model and the conditions required to infer amount from a gas observation.

